          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies judgment as God’s righteous evaluation and verdict, paradigmatically the final eschatological judgment, while recognizing temporal judgment, discipline, vindication, and human discernment as secondary senses.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book treats judgment more broadly as judicial governance: administering justice, discerning right from wrong, and making or executing rulings according to God’s law. Condemnation is one possible result rather than the essence of judgment.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine righteous evaluation resulting in a just verdict, especially at the final judgment.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Law-governed judicial discernment and administration, including the making and execution of rulings.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book places ongoing governance, positive administration of justice, human or royal adjudication, and executed rulings within the central meaning of judgment. The consensus acknowledges many such cases but treats them as secondary to divine eschatological evaluation.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: final divine evaluation remains a valid instance of the book’s broader category. The material difference is that the book rejects treating condemnation or the final tribunal as judgment’s defining center.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings retain the core idea of discerning and rendering justice. The book does not replace that referent but broadens its extension from primarily divine eschatological evaluation to the larger judicial activity of governing, adjudicating, vindicating, and executing rulings. Several consensus alternatives already recognize portions of this broader field, so the entry is not novel.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Because the consensus’s minimum core already includes just evaluation, verdict, vindication, and temporal or human judgment, the difference could be viewed as emphasis rather than extension. Nevertheless, the book’s explicit inclusion of governance and administration as constitutive meanings materially broadens what the consensus presents as the primary category.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
